WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.010
By now, I think we all know about SpaceX and
Blue Origin’s ambitions to create a reliable

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.090
satellite internet service.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:11.329
SpaceX has already launched thousands of starlink
satellites and they are currently in the process

00:00:11.329 --> 00:00:12.969
of rolling out a beta version.

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:17.730
Meanwhile, Blue Origin/Amazon seems to be
years behind as they haven’t even reached

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.090
orbit yet.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.480
Considering either company’s situations,
people love to make fun of Blue Origin for

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.630
making very minimal progress.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:29.429
But what’s ironic though is that both SpaceX
and Blue Origin are decades late to the game

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:33.010
as Bill Gates was trying to launch his own
satellite internet service called Teledesic

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.950
way back in the early 1990s.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.700
So, here’s the story of the time Bill Gates
tried to launch his own starlink but unfortunately

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.270
failed.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:47.640
Teledesic was originally founded in June of
1990 by business mogul Craig McCaw.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.610
Craig had previously hit it big with his company
McCaw Cellular which was a leader within the

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.330
cellular telephone market.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:01:00.130
His father had actually founded the company
way back in 1966 as a cable television provider,

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:04.400
and though he did find a good amount of success
within that industry, it wasn’t till Craig

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.860
shifted the focus of the company to the cellular
market that they became a major player.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:15.970
AT&amp;T would end up buying McCaw Cellular for
$12.6 billion in 1993 making Craig a billionaire.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.750
So clearly, Craig was not messing around when
it came to Teledesic.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.870
He was previously able to identify an upcoming
boom within the cellular market which made

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.490
him a billionaire.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.350
And now, he aimed to do the exact same thing
with the satellite internet market.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:33.310
The first couple of years were pretty slow
as Craig was still very much focused on McCaw

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:34.670
Cellular.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:40.130
But after the acquisition in 1993, Craig would
turn his full focus onto Teledesic and he

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.630
would onboard a notable partner which was
of course Bill Gates.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:50.590
In 1994, both Craig and Bill Gates would invest
$5 million into the company and with that

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:54.900
Craig would begin to pitch his idea to countries
and companies around the world.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.900
But wait a minute, both Craig and Bill were
billionaires with an abundance of connections

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.460
within the business world.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:05.210
So, why did they need to raise money and bring
on investors?

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:09.700
Well, similar to Elon’s vision with Starlink
today, Craig’s vision was simply so massive

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.450
that even two billionaires couldn’t handle
it by themselves.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:18.191
You see, Craig wasn’t looking to launch
10 or 20 satellites, no he wanted to launch

00:02:18.191 --> 00:02:20.480
840 satellites into space.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.409
At the time, this was more than the total
number of satellites in space.

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:28.719
And accordingly, it came with a hefty setup
cost of $9 billion.

00:02:28.719 --> 00:02:32.909
Though the project was extremely expensive,
it promised to make internet access basically

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:34.189
universal.

00:02:34.189 --> 00:02:38.200
No matter how remote a location was, it could
use teledesic’s service to contact people

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.200
in the city.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.769
Given the company’s vision and the people
behind the company, many investors would give

00:02:42.769 --> 00:02:44.220
the company a shot.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.230
Boeing would pledge to invest up to $100 million.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:52.690
Saud Prince Alwaleed Bin Talal would invest
$200 million and Motorola would invest a massive

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.129
$750 million.

00:02:55.129 --> 00:03:00.261
Ironically, the company had no working product,
but it was able to raise nearly a billion

00:03:00.261 --> 00:03:04.689
dollars mostly because Bill Gates and Craig
McCaw were involved.

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:09.269
Aside from raising a crap ton of money, Bill
and Craig were even able to convince governments

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:13.750
to set aside a portion of the electromagnetic
spectrum that would be exclusively used by

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.150
Tesledesic’s network.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:20.400
Clearly, Bill and Craig were not messing around
and now, it was time to execute.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.519
The duo was very much aware of how important
execution was, so they chose the best partner

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:28.420
they could which was of course Boeing.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:32.450
Boeing was given most of the responsibility
of designing the satellites, building the

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.409
satellites, and launching the satellites.

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:38.860
Boeing already had extensive experience working
on space projects, so this project was a piece

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.079
of cake for them.

00:03:40.079 --> 00:03:43.629
Teledesic’s proposed satellites were super
small and didn’t have to be launched that

00:03:43.629 --> 00:03:45.120
far into space.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.620
So, Boeing planned to simply launch clusters
of satellites on large existing boosters which

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.599
is not that different from Starlink missions
today.

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:56.829
While Boeing was working on deploying Teledesic’s
satellites, the relatively small Teledesic

00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:02.450
team of 100 employees focused on getting regulatory
approval and hitting other technical requirements.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.129
Everything seemed to be quickly progressing
forward, but this is when Teledesic would

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:08.420
face its first major setback.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.949
As we previously discussed, Teledesic wanted
to launch 840 super small satellites, but

00:04:13.949 --> 00:04:17.540
Boeing insisted on launching fewer larger
satellites.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:22.400
Given that Boeing was Teledesic’s primary
contractor, Teledesic would agree and reduce

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.289
the proposed number of satellites to just
300.

00:04:25.289 --> 00:04:29.330
The official explanation for this switch was
that Boeing’s proposed plan was superior

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.280
to Teledesics original idea.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.470
However, a former employee of Teledesic would
eventually reveal in 2016 that this explanation

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:37.780
was a complete lie.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:42.539
The primary reason Boeing insisted so heavily
on launching larger satellites is because

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:46.419
legacy satellite manufacturers already had
large satellites in inventory.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:50.680
Now, manufacturing smaller satellites like
Telesdeic wanted wouldn’t be that hard for

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.259
them, but the manufacturers were heavily opposed
to this idea.

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:58.780
You see, these traditional satellite manufacturers
had a monopoly within the industry and it

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.720
was nearly impossible for a startup to compete
against them as the bar for entry was so high.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.160
However, if these manufacturers expanded to
producing smaller satellites, the bar for

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.729
entry would be significantly lowered which
could lead to them losing market share to

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:12.729
startups.

00:05:12.729 --> 00:05:16.310
Aside from this, the manufacturers were also
fearful that if they started to produce smaller

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.790
satellites, the US military would also prefer
to buy the smaller ones which had much lower

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:21.790
profit margins.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:27.050
So, basically, Telesdesic was forced to switch
to larger satellites because satellite manufacturers

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.090
didn’t want to let go of their monopoly.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:33.729
We’re not exactly sure how much these larger
satellites cost, but assuming that the cost

00:05:33.729 --> 00:05:38.099
to launch all the satellites was constant
at $9 billion, each satellite cost approximately

00:05:38.099 --> 00:05:39.940
$30 million.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:45.300
To put this in perspective, Starlink satellites
are expected to cost $250,000 per satellite

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.250
and already cost less than $500,000 per satellite.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:52.110
Now, starlink satellites are more comparable
to the smaller satellites Teledesic wanted

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:56.650
to launch, but even then the cost per satellite
is insanely high.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:03.199
If we spread the $9 billion over 840 satellites,
we get nearly $11 million per satellite which

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:06.650
is over 20 times the cost of a starlink satellite
today.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:11.349
And the truth is satellite costs have not
dropped 20 fold over the past two decades.

00:06:11.349 --> 00:06:16.250
So, Teledesic was more or less getting ripped
off when it came to launching satellites.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:20.330
Aside from getting ripped off, major red flags
started to pop up that people weren’t actually

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:23.030
that interested in a satellite based internet
service.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:28.950
In 1999, two satellite companies named Iridium
communications and ICO would file for bankruptcy.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:32.699
These guys weren’t targeting the satellite
internet market but they were targeting the

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:34.520
satellite phone market, but it clearly didn’t
work out too well for them.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:38.430
At first glance, you might think that maybe
they ran out of funding or that investors

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:42.990
backed away; however, this was actually still
before the dotcom crash.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:45.629
So, funding wasn’t that hard to secure.

00:06:45.629 --> 00:06:48.940
The real problem with these companies was
that there was very little demand for their

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.099
services.

00:06:50.099 --> 00:06:53.849
The only people buying their services were
people working fishing boats and people working

00:06:53.849 --> 00:06:55.050
on oil rigs.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.699
Let’s also keep in mind that during this
time period, having a cellphone connection

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:01.990
was a much higher priority than having an
internet connection.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:06.389
So, if these cell phone companies couldn’t
find much demand, it was unlikely that Teledesic

00:07:06.389 --> 00:07:08.919
would find much demand with the internet.

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:13.909
And this makes sense, providing internet access
to every corner of the world is a very noble

00:07:13.909 --> 00:07:18.580
cause, but how many people living in rural
villages in the late 1990s wanted to access

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.479
the internet, had a device that could access
the internet, and could afford to pay for

00:07:22.479 --> 00:07:23.710
a monthly internet subscription.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:28.490
From an outside perspective, it’s pretty
clear why there was lackluster demand, but

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:31.159
Craig didn’t really see this side of the
picture.

00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:36.680
In fact, he actually thought that the bankruptcies
of Iridium and ICO were great opportunities,

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.169
and he would spend the next couple of years
trying to acquire these two companies.

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:45.569
At one point, he would even invest $74.6 million
into Iridium to keep it afloat.

00:07:45.569 --> 00:07:49.310
Clearly, the other investors of Teledesic
weren’t thrilled to see McCaw focusing on

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.470
keeping these random companies alive instead
of focusing on teledesic.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:57.840
This would even lead to an investor filing
a lawsuit against McCaw for using Teledesic’s

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.050
resources to aid in his personal interest
in ICO.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:05.279
Things were falling apart rapidly and Teledesic
was yet to even launch a single satellite,

00:08:05.279 --> 00:08:11.000
and this is when the dotcom crash would happen.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:15.370
Investors were losing millions of dollars
right and left, and they had no intention

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.590
of continuing to fund Teledesic.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:21.759
Meanwhile, Bill Gates had just stepped down
from his role as Microsoft CEO and he was

00:08:21.759 --> 00:08:23.699
looking to focus on philanthropy.

00:08:23.699 --> 00:08:28.120
He really had no interest in trying to grow
another startup, so he didn’t do much to

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.120
save the company.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:32.070
Fortunately though, Teledesic had not yet
locked in on a satellite contract with any

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:33.070
company.

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:37.320
They did originally work with Boeing, but
then they switched to Motorola and eventually

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.540
they switched to Thales Alenia Space.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:43.000
And, ironically, their constant switching
would turn out to be a good thing.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.520
Since they hadn’t locked into a contract
with any of these companies, they actually

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.060
still had a lot of the cash they raised.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:52.540
Considering this unique situation, investors
would decide to pull the plug when they still

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:53.560
could.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:56.960
And given the structure in which money is
raised for startups, early investors would

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.630
actually end up profiting from their investment
in Teledesic despite the company not doing

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:01.770
anything.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:06.530
Late investors, however, would lose money,
but even those losses were quite minimal especially

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:10.110
compared to many companies that closed down
during the dotcom crash.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:14.880
At the end of the day, Teledesic aimed to
revolutionize global internet, but honestly

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:19.080
it was just way too early as in decades too
early.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:23.280
Combine this with the fact that Teledesic
didn’t have launch capability in house and

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.340
the fact that Teledesic was more of a side
project for McCaw and Gates, and it’s not

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:30.100
hard to see why the project never worked out.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:34.380
It is, however, insane to think that these
two geniuses had the idea to basically launch

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:36.670
starlink 30 years ago.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:38.290
Did you guys know about Teledesic?

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:39.840
Comment that down below.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.770
Also drop a like if you guys are confident
that Starlink will have a much better fate.

